3.1 What is Polymorphism?

Definition: Polymorphism is an OOP concept in which the same method name
or reference can represent different behaviors in different situations.

The word polymorphism comes from two words:
- Poly = Many
- Morphism = Forms

So, polymorphism means many forms.
Real-life example

Consider a person who can perform different roles:
- At home, they are a family member.
- At college, they are a student.
- At work, they are an employee.

The same person has different roles in different situations.
In Java, polymorphism allows methods or objects to exhibit different behaviors
depending on how they are used.

Java example
class Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal makes a sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}

class Cat extends Animal {
    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Cat meows");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Dog d = new Dog();
        Cat c = new Cat();

        d.sound();
        c.sound();
    }
}


Output
Dog barks
Cat meows


Both classes have a sound() method, but each provides different behavior.





3.2 Types of Polymorphism
Java commonly teaches two main types of polymorphism.

1. Compile-time polymorphism
Method overloading
The compiler determines which overloaded method to call based on the method
arguments and applicable overload-resolution rules.


2. Runtime polymorphism
Method overriding
For an overridden instance method, Java selects the implementation based on
the actual object's class at runtime.





3.3 Compile-time Polymorphism
Compile-time polymorphism is commonly achieved through method overloading.

What is method overloading?
Method overloading means defining multiple methods with the same name in the
same class but with different parameter lists.

The parameter lists can differ by:
- Number of parameters.
- Types of parameters.
- Order of parameter types.

Example 1: Different number of parameters
class Calculator {

    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    int add(int a, int b, int c) {
        return a + b + c;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Calculator c = new Calculator();

        System.out.println(c.add(10, 20));
        System.out.println(c.add(10, 20, 30));
    }
}


Output
30
60


Explanation
- add(int a, int b) accepts two arguments.
- add(int a, int b, int c) accepts three arguments.
- Java selects the applicable method based on the arguments.


Example 2: Different parameter types

class Calculator {

    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    double add(double a, double b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Calculator c = new Calculator();

        System.out.println(c.add(10, 20));
        System.out.println(c.add(10.5, 20.5));
    }
}


Output
30
31.0


The first call uses the int version, while the second uses the double version.

Example 3: Different order of parameter types
class Display {

    void show(int a, String b) {
        System.out.println(a + " " + b);
    }

    void show(String a, int b) {
        System.out.println(a + " " + b);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Display d = new Display();

        d.show(10, "Java");
        d.show("Java", 20);
    }
}


Output
10 Java
Java 20


The parameter types are the same, but their order differs, so these are
valid overloads.

Important rules of method overloading
Rule 1: The method name must be the same.
Rule 2: The parameter list must be different.
Rule 3: Changing only the return type is not enough.

Invalid example:
int calculate(int a) {
    return a;
}

// Compilation error: same signature
double calculate(int a) {
    return a;
}


Rule 4: Overloading can happen in the same class or across inherited methods.
Rule 5: Static methods can also be overloaded.
Rule 6: Constructors can be overloaded, as you learned in the previous module.





3.4 Runtime Polymorphism

Runtime polymorphism is commonly achieved through method overriding.

What is method overriding?

Method overriding occurs when a subclass provides its own implementation of
an inherited instance method with the same signature.

Example
class Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal makes a sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal a = new Dog();
        a.sound();
    }
}


Output
Dog barks


Why does this happen?
Look at this line:
Animal a = new Dog();


It has two important parts:
- Animal is the reference type.
- new Dog() creates a Dog object.
When Java executes:
a.sound();


it checks the actual object at runtime. Since the object is a Dog and Dog
overrides sound(), the Dog implementation runs.

This is runtime polymorphism.


Remember: The reference type determines which members can be accessed at
compile time, while the actual object's class determines which overridden
instance method implementation runs at runtime.




