3.6 Dynamic Method Dispatch

Dynamic method dispatch is the mechanism Java uses to select the overridden
instance method implementation at runtime.

Example
class Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}

class Cat extends Animal {
    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Cat meows");
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Animal a;

        a = new Dog();
        a.sound();   // Dynamic method dispatch → Dog's sound()

        a = new Cat();
        a.sound();   // Dynamic method dispatch → Cat's sound()

        a = new Animal();
        a.sound();  // Animal's sound()
    }
}


Output
Dog barks
Cat meows
Animal sound


Execution flow
Animal a
Parent-type reference

a = new Dog()
Actual object: Dog

a.sound()
Dog's implementation runs


The same reference variable can refer to different subclass objects at different times.





3.7 Upcasting

Upcasting means treating a child-class object as a parent-class reference.

It is allowed implicitly.

Example
class Animal {

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Animal eats");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    void bark() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal a = new Dog();
        a.eat();
        // a.bark(); // Compilation error
    }
}


Output
Animal eats


Although the actual object is a Dog, the reference type is Animal.

Therefore, you can directly call members accessible through the Animal type,
but not Dog-specific methods through a.

To call bark(), you need a suitable Dog reference.




3.8 Down-casting

Downcasting means converting a parent-class reference into a child-class reference.
It requires an explicit cast.
Example
class Animal {

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Animal eats");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {

    void bark() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Animal a = new Dog();
        Dog d = (Dog) a;

        d.eat();
        d.bark();
    }
}


Output
Animal eats
Dog barks


The cast works because the actual object referenced by a is a Dog.




Special Cases
Static Methods

Static methods are hidden, not overridden. A static method call is resolved
using the compile-time type.

class Parent {
    static void show() {
        System.out.println("Parent");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    static void show() {
        System.out.println("Child");
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Parent p = new Child();
        p.show();
    }
}

Output:

Parent

Private Methods
Private methods cannot be overridden because they are not inherited by
subclasses in the normal overriding sense. They do not participate in
dynamic method dispatch through overriding.

Final Methods
A final instance method cannot be overridden. Therefore, a subclass cannot
provide a different implementation for dynamic dispatch to select.





3.10 Rules of Method Overriding
Remember these interview rules:
1. The subclass method must have the same signature as the inherited method.
2. The return type must be identical or covariant.
3. The overriding method cannot reduce the visibility of the inherited method.
4. A final method cannot be overridden.
5. Static methods are hidden, not overridden.
6. Private methods are not inherited and cannot be overridden.
7. Constructors cannot be overridden.
8. The @Override annotation helps the compiler verify your intention.
9. An overriding method may declare fewer or narrower checked exceptions,
   but cannot introduce broader checked exceptions than allowed by the
   overridden method.



3.11 Real-World Example: Payment System

Polymorphism is useful when different classes perform the same operation
differently.

class Payment {

    void pay(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Processing generic payment");
    }
}

class UpiPayment extends Payment {

    @Override
    void pay(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Paid ₹" + amount + " using UPI");
    }
}

class CardPayment extends Payment {

    @Override
    void pay(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Paid ₹" + amount + " using Card");
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Payment p;

        p = new UpiPayment();
        p.pay(500);

        p = new CardPayment();
        p.pay(1000);
    }
}


Output
Paid ₹500.0 using UPI
Paid ₹1000.0 using Card


The calling code uses the same pay() method, but the actual payment object
determines the behavior. This makes it easier to add new payment methods
without rewriting the calling logic.



